## 5. Content & copy guidelines

- Write all copy in plain English. Avoid legal jargon entirely — the visitor is not a lawyer.
- Tone: warm, calm, human, confident. Avoid corporate stiffness ("Our firm leverages decades of experience...") and avoid over-familiar casualness (no slang, no exclamation-point-heavy copy).
- Every section should logically earn its place in the trust-building arc: emotional connection → relevance/expertise → process clarity → human credibility → social proof → objection handling → action. Do not reorder sections without reconsidering this arc.
- The primary CTA wording should be IDENTICAL everywhere it appears (e.g. always "Book your free consultation" — not swapped between "Get started," "Contact us," "Schedule now," etc.). Consistency reduces cognitive friction.

---

## 6. What NOT to do (explicit anti-patterns to avoid)

- Do NOT use a purple-to-blue gradient anywhere, or any gradient at all.
- Do NOT default to Inter font.
- Do NOT use navy + gold, or black + gold — this is the generic law firm cliché we are deliberately avoiding.
- Do NOT use stock imagery of gavels, scales of justice, courtroom benches, or handshake-over-desk clichés.
- Do NOT introduce a second/third accent color beyond the sage system defined above.
- Do NOT build a multi-page site or add routing — this is a single scrolling page.
- Do NOT build custom calendar/booking backend logic — the Calendly embed handles this.
- Do NOT use generic shadcn/MUI default component styling without restyling it to match this design system — components must look custom-designed, not library-default.
- Do NOT use ALL CAPS headlines or aggressive, urgency-driven copy ("ACT NOW," "LIMITED TIME") — this directly contradicts the "not ambulance-chaser" brand requirement.

---

## 7. Responsive & accessibility requirements

- Fully responsive, mobile-first. Test at 375px, 768px, and 1280px+ widths minimum.
- All interactive elements (buttons, accordion triggers, nav links) must have visible focus states and sufficient tap target size (min 44x44px) for mobile.
- Color contrast: ensure `charcoal` text on `cream`/`sand` backgrounds, and `cream` text on `sage`/`sage-dark` backgrounds, meet at least WCAG AA contrast standards.
- Semantic HTML: proper heading hierarchy (one h1, logical h2/h3 nesting), `<nav>`, `<section>`, `<footer>` landmarks, alt text on all images/icons that convey meaning.

---

## 8. Deliverable

- A working, deployed build (Vercel/Netlify) or a local dev server that runs cleanly with `npm install && npm run dev`.
- Clean component structure — one component per section (e.g. `Hero.tsx`, `PracticeAreas.tsx`, `Team.tsx`, `HowItWorks.tsx`, `Testimonials.tsx`, `FAQ.tsx`, `FinalCTA.tsx`, `Footer.tsx`, `Header.tsx`) assembled in `App.tsx` in the order defined in Section 4.
- No unused boilerplate from the Vite starter template (default Vite logos, sample counter component, etc.) left in the final build.